{"id":938,"date":"2025-05-13T14:22:42","date_gmt":"2025-05-13T12:22:42","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/menorcas-almost-secret-beaches-a-locals-guide-with-naturist-spots-and-types-of-sand\/"},"modified":"2026-07-02T13:44:15","modified_gmt":"2026-07-02T11:44:15","slug":"discover-menorcas-secret-beaches-with-daniel-vaquera","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/discover-menorcas-secret-beaches-with-daniel-vaquera\/","title":{"rendered":"Menorca&#8217;s (almost) Secret Beaches: a Local&#8217;s Guide with Naturist Spots and Types of Sand"},"content":{"rendered":"<article>\n<figure>\n<img fetchpriority=\"high\" src=\"https:\/\/commons.wikimedia.org\/wiki\/Special:FilePath\/Cala%20Escorxada%20Migjorn.jpg?width=1600\" alt=\"Virgin cove and cliffs with turquoise waters in Menorca\" width=\"1600\" height=\"1000\" loading=\"eager\" decoding=\"async\"><br \/>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Menorca hides beaches you won\u2019t find in the brochures. Getting there means a bit of walking \u2014that\u2019s why they\u2019re still untouched\u2014 but the reward is huge: silence, crystal-clear water, and fine sand. I\u2019m <strong>Daniel Vaquera<\/strong>, a local official guide at Voyager Menorca, and I\u2019ve been walking these trails for years in every season. This is my straight, practical guide to discovering four gems with <strong>real routes, timings, and tips<\/strong>, plus the context that helps you understand each cove: why the sand is that colour, where the names come from, and which day is best to visit each one. If you\u2019d rather play it safe, you can book a <a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/field-trips\/\">private guided tour<\/a> with me.    <\/p>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/wa.me\/34649902301?text=Hola%20Dani%2C%20quiero%20informaci%C3%B3n%20sobre%20las%20playas%20secretas%20de%20Menorca\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>\ud83d\udcf2 WhatsApp Dani<\/strong><\/a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/field-trips\/\">See excursions<\/a><\/p>\n<p><em>Best: May\u2013October \u00b7 Difficulty: easy\u2013moderate \u00b7 Adventurous families<\/em><\/p>\n<p><em>Note on naturism:<\/em> these unspoilt coves are traditionally mixed and <strong>naturist-friendly<\/strong>, especially Escorxada, Fustam and Pilar. Everyone coexists naturally and respectfully: the unwritten rule is mutual discretion. <\/p>\n<nav aria-label=\"Contenido\">\n<h2>Content<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"#cala-escorxada\">Cala Escorxada<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#cala-fustam\">Cala Fustam<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#itinerario-sur\">Mini south itinerary: Mitjana \u2192 Fustam \u2192 Escorxada<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#cala-pilar\">Cala Pilar<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#cala-pregonda\">Cala Pregonda<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#tipos-arena\">Sand types: white, golden and reddish<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#que-cala-hoy\">Which cove should I choose today? The wind rule <\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#consejos\">Tips and what to bring<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#faq\">Frequently Asked Questions<\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/nav>\n<section id=\"cala-escorxada\">\n<h2>Cala Escorxada: the wildest postcard on the Migjorn<\/h2>\n<p>An unspoilt cove with fine white sand, turquoise water, and an amphitheatre of mar\u00e9s cliffs crowned with pines. No beach bars or sun loungers: just posidonia, silence, and the scent of resin drifting down from the pinewood. As in almost all the southern coves, a <strong>ravine<\/strong> carved over millennia into the limestone platform ends here, and that ravine is the reason the beach exists: sand builds up right where the stream meets the sea.  <\/p>\n<p>On calm-sea days, <em>snorkelling<\/em> along the side walls is spectacular: seabream, wrasse and, if you\u2019re lucky, a painted comber among the posidonia patches. Precisely because of that meadow \u2014the one that keeps the water so clear\u2014 it\u2019s best not to anchor on top of it or pull away the dry remains on the shore: dead posidonia protects the sand from winter storms. <\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Access:<\/strong> <a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/tourist-guide-cami-de-cavalls\/\">Cam\u00ed de Cavalls<\/a> from Cala Mitjana (westbound) or from Sant Tom\u00e0s via Binigaus (eastbound, longer).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Walking time:<\/strong> 45\u201360 min from Mitjana along a dirt path with intermittent pine shade.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Services:<\/strong> none (bring water and your own shade).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Ideal for:<\/strong> couples, snorkellers and laid-back travellers.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<figure>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/commons.wikimedia.org\/wiki\/Special:FilePath\/CalaEscorxada%2002.jpg?width=1400\" alt=\"Cala Escorxada in Menorca with white sand and turquoise water\" width=\"1400\" height=\"933\"><figcaption>Cala Escorxada, south coast of Menorca. Photo: Alejandro Mu\u00f1oz Cabrisas \/ Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). <\/figcaption><\/figure>\n<\/section>\n<section id=\"cala-fustam\">\n<figure>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/commons.wikimedia.org\/wiki\/Special:FilePath\/2007_06_30_-_Fustam_01_(7381602102).jpg?width=1400\" alt=\"Cala Fustam, small virgin cove in Menorca surrounded by pine trees\" width=\"1400\" height=\"933\"><figcaption>The tranquility of Cala Fustam. Photo: Boladevidre (Flickr) via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 2.0). <\/figcaption><\/figure>\n<h2>Cala Fustam: small, shady and very quiet<\/h2>\n<p>Next to Escorxada \u2014they\u2019re separated by just one headland\u2014 Fustam is even more intimate: barely 30 metres of sand tucked between rock and pinewood. Its name comes from wood (<em>fusta<\/em> in Catalan): for centuries, coves like this served as natural landing spots to load firewood and timber from inland forests onto coastal boats. Today the pinewood remains, offering <strong>proper natural shade<\/strong> \u2014a rare luxury in unspoilt coves\u2014 and a small cave on the side that kids love.  <\/p>\n<p>The water is usually well sheltered from southerly winds and stays calm when other Migjorn coves have swell. Perfect for a long, unhurried swim. <\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Access:<\/strong> Cam\u00ed de Cavalls from Mitjana, passing Escorxada first.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Walking time:<\/strong> 60\u201375 min total from the Mitjana car park.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Services:<\/strong> none. Patchy mobile coverage. <\/li>\n<li><strong>Recommendation:<\/strong> start early; the pine shade in the morning makes the walk much more pleasant.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/section>\n<section id=\"itinerario-sur\">\n<h2>Mini south itinerary: Mitjana \u2192 Fustam \u2192 Escorxada (a perfect half day)<\/h2>\n<p>If you only have one morning, this combo is unbeatable and it\u2019s the one I usually suggest on my guided outings:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>8:30<\/strong> \u2014 Park at the Cala Mitjana car park (arrive early: it fills up). Quick first swim in Mitjana or Mitjaneta with the cove almost empty. <\/li>\n<li><strong>9:30<\/strong> \u2014 Cam\u00ed de Cavalls westbound: gentle climb through pines, viewpoints over the cliffs, and down to <strong>Escorxada<\/strong> (~50 min).<\/li>\n<li><strong>11:00<\/strong> \u2014 Short hop to <strong>Fustam<\/strong> (another 10\u201315 min). Long swim, pine shade and a picnic. <\/li>\n<li><strong>13:30<\/strong> \u2014 Easy return. Total: about 6\u20137 km, suitable for anyone used to walking. <\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>With small kids, plan it only as far as Escorxada; with teenagers, the full route works well. And if the sea is calm, bring your snorkel mask: the rocky stretches between coves are like an aquarium. <\/p>\n<\/section>\n<section id=\"cala-pilar\">\n<h2>Cala Pilar: the wildest north face<\/h2>\n<p>We switch worlds. In Pilar the sand is golden with reddish streaks, dunes climb up the hillside, and the landscape feels like another planet: here the <strong>clays and slates of the north of the island<\/strong> crop out, Menorca\u2019s oldest materials. The contrast with the white southern coves is so dramatic that on my routes I often say crossing the island is like travelling 300 million years in half an hour by car.  <\/p>\n<p>The access walk crosses a shady holm-oak wood \u2014rare on the coast\u2014 and ends at a viewpoint where the cove suddenly appears, with the islet of <em>Ses Salines<\/em> in the background. With tramontana there\u2019s serious swell: that day, the south is better. With a southerly wind, though, Pilar is a mirror.  <\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Access:<\/strong> signposted trail from the Cam\u00ed del Pilar car park (Me-1 road, turn-off at Alfur\u00ed).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Walking time:<\/strong> 30\u201340 min with elevation (the return is uphill).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Services:<\/strong> none. Closed-toe shoes essential. <\/li>\n<li><strong>Ideal:<\/strong> nature lovers, geology fans and photographers.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<figure>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/commons.wikimedia.org\/wiki\/Special:FilePath\/Cala_Pilar.jpg?width=1400\" alt=\"Cala Pilar on the north coast of Menorca with golden sand and reddish rocks\" width=\"1400\" height=\"933\"><figcaption>The mineral landscape of Cala Pilar. Photo: Alejandro Mu\u00f1oz Cabrisas \/ Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). <\/figcaption><\/figure>\n<\/section>\n<section id=\"cala-pregonda\">\n<figure>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https:\/\/commons.wikimedia.org\/wiki\/Special:FilePath\/Cala%20Pregonda.jpg?width=1400\" alt=\"Cala Pregonda with rocky islets and orange sand in Menorca\" width=\"1400\" height=\"933\"><figcaption>Islets that protect Cala Pregonda: clear waters all summer. Photo: MontanNito \/ Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0). <\/figcaption><\/figure>\n<h2>Cala Pregonda: reddish sand and calm waters<\/h2>\n<p>The classic of the north. Its name probably comes from <em>pregon<\/em>, \u201cdeep\u201d in the island\u2019s old Catalan: the bay opens wide, deep and sheltered, with a string of islets that soften the swell and create a <strong>huge natural pool<\/strong>. The orange colour of the sand comes from the breakdown of local clays and sandstones, and at sunset the whole cove lights up in copper tones that look like Mars.  <\/p>\n<p>The walk from Binimel\u00b7l\u00e0 is the gentlest on this list and gives you views the whole way, with the old salt pans and the islets on the horizon. <em>Snorkelling<\/em> among the islets is some of the best in Menorca: mixed rocky and sandy seabeds with lots of life. <\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Access:<\/strong> from the Binimel\u00b7l\u00e0 car park via the marked Cam\u00ed de Cavalls trail.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Walking time:<\/strong> 20\u201330 min, easy.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Services:<\/strong> restaurant in Binimel\u00b7l\u00e0 \u2014 none on the cove itself.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Snorkel:<\/strong> excellent among the islets.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/section>\n<section id=\"tipos-arena\">\n<h2>Sand types: white, golden and reddish<\/h2>\n<p>Menorca\u2019s geology splits the island into two worlds, and the beaches tell the story better than any book:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fine white sand (south):<\/strong> born from the Migjorn limestone platform, \u201cyoung\u201d Miocene Menorca (around 20 million years old). Escorxada, Fustam, Mitjana or Macarella are its daughters: turquoise water over a pale seabed, the \u201cCaribbean\u201d effect in photos. <\/li>\n<li><strong>Golden sand (north and west):<\/strong> a mix of sandstones and stream deposits. La Vall\/Algaiarens is the great example. <\/li>\n<li><strong>Reddish sand (north):<\/strong> Tramuntana clays and slates, the oldest materials in the Balearics (up to 400 million years old, from the Paleozoic). Pregonda, Cavalleria and Pilar owe their colour to this primitive Menorca. <\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>This geological contrast \u2014two islands in one\u2014 is one of the reasons Menorca has been a <a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/menorca-biosphere-reserve\/\">Biosphere Reserve<\/a> since 1993.<\/p>\n<\/section>\n<section id=\"que-cala-hoy\">\n<h2>Which cove should I choose today? The wind rule <\/h2>\n<p>In Menorca, the wind decides the plan \u2014and if you understand it, you\u2019ll always get it right:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tramontana (north):<\/strong> the north turns rough \u2192 go south: <strong>Escorxada and Fustam<\/strong> will be sheltered and jellyfish-free.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Migjorn (south):<\/strong> the south gets murky \u2192 go north: <strong>Pregonda and Pilar<\/strong> like mirrors.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Dead calm:<\/strong> any of the four; choose based on the walk you feel like doing.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>It\u2019s the same rule I explain in depth in the <a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/las-mejores-playas-de-menorca-y-cuales-evitar-en-temporada-alta\/\">general beach guide<\/a>, with a wind table and the anti-jellyfish trick included.<\/p>\n<\/section>\n<section id=\"consejos\">\n<h2>Tips and what to bring<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Sports or hiking shoes. Avoid flip-flops for access. <\/li>\n<li>Water (min. 1.5 L per person), fruit, and something salty. There are no beach bars. <\/li>\n<li>Sun protection: cap, sunglasses and <strong>biodegradable<\/strong> sunscreen (the posidonia will thank you).<\/li>\n<li>Light towel and bag for your trash. <strong>Leave the cove better than you found it.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li>Mask and snorkel: in these four coves, they\u2019re always worth it.<\/li>\n<li>Let someone know your plan: coverage is patchy and in the low season you might be alone.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/section>\n<section id=\"faq\">\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions<\/h2>\n<h3>Can I go by public transport?<\/h3>\n<p>In summer there are buses to <em>Cala Galdana<\/em> (to access Mitjana\/Fustam\/Escorxada) and to <em>Es Mercadal<\/em> or <em>Ferreries<\/em> for the north, although the last few kilometres to the northern car parks require a taxi or car. Check updated timetables before you go. <\/p>\n<h3>Is there mobile coverage?<\/h3>\n<p>Variable and sometimes limited in virgin coves. Tell someone your plan and check the weather before leaving. <\/p>\n<h3>Are they nudist beaches?<\/h3>\n<p>There\u2019s no official signage: they\u2019re unspoilt coves with a mixed vibe where naturism is common and accepted, especially in Escorxada, Fustam and Pilar. Coexistence is natural and respectful. <\/p>\n<h3>What\u2019s the best time of year?<\/h3>\n<p>May to October for swimming. June and September are the sweet spot: great water, long light, and quiet trails. In peak August, start early: at 9:00 you\u2019ll have the cove almost to yourself; at 12:00, you won\u2019t.  <\/p>\n<h3>Can I book a guided tour?<\/h3>\n<p>Yes. I organise private routes tailored to your pace and level, choosing the coast based on the day\u2019s wind. Message me on WhatsApp or check out <a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/field-trips\/\">our excursions<\/a>.  <\/p>\n<\/section>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/wa.me\/34649902301?text=Hola%20Dani%2C%20quiero%20reservar%20una%20visita%20a%20las%20playas%20secretas\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\"><strong>Book via WhatsApp<\/strong><\/a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/field-trips\/\">See excursions<\/a><\/p>\n<h2>More Menorca guides<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/las-mejores-playas-de-menorca-y-cuales-evitar-en-temporada-alta\/\">The best beaches in Menorca (and which ones to avoid) \u2192<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/tourist-guide-cami-de-cavalls\/\">Cam\u00ed de Cavalls Guide \u2192<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/sunset-menorca-9-viewpoints\/\">The 9 best viewpoints for sunset \u2192<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/menorca-en-septiembre\/\">Menorca in September \u2192<\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p style=\"opacity:.8\"><em>Official guide in Menorca \u00b7 Daniel Vaquera \u00b7 APITMe<\/em><\/p>\n<\/article>\n<p><script type=\"application\/ld+json\">\n{\n  \"@context\": \"https:\/\/schema.org\",\n  \"@graph\": [\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"BlogPosting\",\n      \"mainEntityOfPage\": { \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"@id\": \"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/descubre-las-playas-secretas-de-menorca-con-daniel-vaquera\/\" },\n      \"headline\": \"Playas (casi) secretas de Menorca: gu\u00eda local con rincones naturistas y tipos de arena\",\n      \"description\": \"Gu\u00eda local de calas v\u00edrgenes de Menorca: Escorxada, Fustam, Pilar y Pregonda. Accesos a pie, tiempos reales, mini-itinerario del sur, tipos de arena y ambiente naturista, por un gu\u00eda oficial de la isla.\",\n      \"image\": \"https:\/\/commons.wikimedia.org\/wiki\/Special:FilePath\/Cala%20Escorxada%20Migjorn.jpg?width=1600\",\n      \"author\": { \"@type\": \"Person\", \"name\": \"Daniel Vaquera\", \"jobTitle\": \"Gu\u00eda oficial de turismo de Menorca\", \"worksFor\": { \"@type\": \"Organization\", \"name\": \"Voyager Menorca\", \"url\": \"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/\" } },\n      \"publisher\": { \"@type\": \"Organization\", \"name\": \"Voyager Menorca\", \"logo\": { \"@type\": \"ImageObject\", \"url\": \"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/logo.png\" } },\n      \"datePublished\": \"2025-05-13\",\n      \"dateModified\": \"2026-07-02\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"FAQPage\",\n      \"mainEntity\": [\n        { \"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"\u00bfPuedo llegar a las playas secretas de Menorca en transporte p\u00fablico?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": { \"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"En verano hay buses a Cala Galdana (para Mitjana, Fustam y Escorxada) y a Es Mercadal o Ferreries para el norte; los \u00faltimos kil\u00f3metros del norte requieren taxi o coche.\" } },\n        { \"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"\u00bfSon playas nudistas?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": { \"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"No hay se\u00f1alizaci\u00f3n oficial: s